# SejiloChat (Flutter client) — configuration
#
# Nothing in this app reads a .env file. There is no flutter_dotenv dependency
# and no code under lib/ opens one, so a value written here has no effect. The
# file is kept only to say that, and to name the one setting that does exist.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The only client-side setting: which backend to talk to
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Resolved in this order (lib/core/api_config.dart):
#   1. the in-app override — Settings → Privacy → server URL
#   2. the compile-time define below
#   3. the hosted instance from render.yaml (the default in a plain build)
#
# So `flutter build apk` already produces an app that talks to the deployed
# backend. Pass the define only to point a build somewhere else:
#
#   flutter run --dart-define=SEJILO_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.20:8080
#
# The WebSocket URL is derived from the same base, so wss:// needs no separate
# setting.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Why there are no credentials in this file
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Anything shipped in an APK is readable by anyone holding the APK, so a secret
# placed here would be published, not configured. Every credential this project
# uses lives on the server instead — in the Render dashboard for the hosted
# deployment, or in backend/.env for a local one. See docs/FREE_HOSTING.md.
#
#   Google sign-in     The app sends the Google ID token to the backend, which
#                      verifies its signature and audience. The client *id* is
#                      public and is compiled in (see
#                      lib/auth/account_auth_controller.dart); there is no
#                      client secret in this design, because no
#                      authorization-code exchange ever happens. If a secret has
#                      been pasted anywhere, rotate it — nothing here needs it.
#                      docs/GOOGLE_SIGNIN.md covers the whole flow.
#   Push notifications android/app/google-services.json, which is generated by
#                      the Firebase console and is not a secret. The FCM service
#                      account stays on the server (FCM_* variables).
#   Media uploads      CLOUDINARY_URL, server-side only. The app uploads through
#                      the backend and never holds a storage credential.
#   SMS / email        SMS_* and RESEND_API_KEY, server-side only.
